// error[E0373]: closure may outlive the current function, but it borrows `v`,
//               which is owned by the current function
// note: function requires argument type to outlive `'static`
// help: to force the closure to take ownership of `v` (and any other
//       referenced variables), use the `move` keyword

use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let handle = thread::spawn(move || {   // v moves into the thread
        let total: i32 = v.iter().sum();   // add up the elements
        (v, total)                         // hand v back, plus a result
    });
    // println!("{v:?}");                  // error[E0382]: v was moved
    let (v, total) = handle.join().unwrap();
    println!("{v:?} {total}");             // [1, 2, 3] 6
}

use std::sync::mpsc;
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();
    thread::spawn(move || {
        let msg = String::from("hi");
        tx.send(msg).unwrap();      // msg moves into the channel
        // println!("{msg}");       // error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: `msg`
    });
    let got: String = rx.recv().unwrap(); // main now owns the String
    println!("got {got}");                // got hi
}

blocks.md:
And two threads changing the same value, the data race from lesson 4? The rules you know refuse it. A plain spawned closure cannot borrow a local at all (E0373 above). `std::thread::scope` can, because it joins every thread it started before it returns, and there shared XOR mutable applies across threads exactly as on one:

```
let mut counter = 0;
thread::scope(|s| {
    s.spawn(|| counter += 1);
    s.spawn(|| counter += 1); // error[E0499]
});
```

Both closures need `&mut counter` while both threads may run: two live mutable borrows, so rustc reports E0499. Sharing needs types built for it: `Arc<T>` (lesson 9's thread-safe Rc) for several owners and `Mutex<T>` for one writer at a time. The compiler tracks which types may cross threads with two marker traits, `Send` and `Sync`, which is why an `Rc` moved into a thread is rejected (E0277). Those types are the subject of a later course that builds on this one (*C and Rust*).
